#

assertion  XSH.nan.001
anchor     functions/nan.html#DESCRIPTION
anchor     functions/nan.html#RETURN VALUE
class      required
since      2001
says       nan(""), nanf("") and nanl("") return a NaN (the same representation
           that strtod(), strtof() and strtold() give for "NAN()") when quiet
           NaNs are supported, and zero when they are not.
test       t/001-empty.c

assertion  XSH.nan.002
anchor     functions/nan.html#DESCRIPTION
anchor     functions/nan.html#RETURN VALUE
class      required
since      2001
says       nan("1"), nan("abc"), nan("A_9") and nan("0x1f") (and the f and l
           forms) return a NaN equivalent to the matching strtod(), strtof() or
           strtold() call on "NAN(" tag ")", when quiet NaNs are supported.
test       t/002-n-char.c

assertion  XSH.nan.003
anchor     functions/nan.html#DESCRIPTION
anchor     functions/nan.html#RETURN VALUE
class      required
since      2001
says       nan("!") and nan("a b") (and the f and l forms), which are not n-char
           sequences, return a NaN equivalent to the matching strtod(), strtof()
           or strtold() call on "NAN".
test       t/003-not-n-char.c

assertion  XSH.nan.004
anchor     functions/nan.html#DESCRIPTION
anchor     functions/nan.html#RETURN VALUE
class      required
since      2001
says       The NaN from nan("") (and the f and l forms) is quiet: adding 1 to it
           raises no invalid exception.
note       The tag on this passage is the IEC 60559 Floating-Point option; the
           test is NOTINUSE unless the C implementation claims it (Annex F of the
           C standard).
test       t/004-quiet.c
